# ---------------- motivations and literature context
E("E040","rationale_stated","Motivation: creating a large training corpus is time-consuming and expensive, hence many retrieval systems are applied zero-shot without training data.","line 11",notes="'creating a large training corpus is often time-consuming and expensive and hence many retrieval systems are applied in a zero-shot setup, with no available training data to train the system.'")
E("E041","rationale_stated","Motivation: it is unclear how well trained neural retrieval models perform on other text domains or tasks, and how sparse versus dense embeddings behave on out-of-distribution data.","line 120",notes="'it is unclear how well existing trained neural models will perform for other text domains or textual retrieval tasks. ... it is unclear how well different approaches, like sparse embeddings vs. dense embeddings, generalize to out-of-distribution data.'")
E("E042","rationale_stated","Motivation for BEIR: prior retrieval benchmarks are narrow, focusing on a single task or a certain domain; neural retrievers have been studied in homogeneous and narrow settings, limiting insight into out-of-distribution behaviour.","lines 7,121",notes="'Prior retrieval benchmarks [19, 50] have issues of a comparatively narrow evaluation focusing either only on a single task, like question-answering, or on a certain domain.'")
E("E043","external_fact","In most prior work neural retrievers are evaluated on the same dataset they are trained on (e.g. Natural Questions, 133k training examples; MS MARCO, 533k), where gains over BM25 are reported.","line 10",notes="'In most prior work, approaches are afterward evaluated on the same dataset, where significant performance gains over lexical approaches like BM25 are demonstrated [15, 31, 46].' (the paper's own text; not verified against those sources)")
E("E044","external_fact","Characterisation of prior benchmarks as given in the paper: MultiReQA has eight QA datasets, sentence-level retrieval, five of eight from Wikipedia, six of eight with fewer than 100k candidate sentences; KILT has five knowledge-intensive tasks with eleven datasets, retrieval is not its primary task and it retrieves only from Wikipedia.","lines 127-131",notes="Description is the paper's own; the cited works were not read.")
E("E045","external_fact","Retrieval is a first step in open-domain question answering, claim verification and duplicate-question detection; retrieval was traditionally dominated by lexical approaches such as TF-IDF and BM25, which suffer from the lexical gap and treat text as a bag of words.","lines 9,133",notes="'these approaches suffer from lexical gap [5] and are able to only retrieve documents containing keywords present within the query.'")
E("E046","external_fact","Neural approaches as described in the paper: document expansion (docT5query) and term weighting (DeepCT, SPARTA) keep an inverted index; dense retrievers embed queries and documents in a shared vector space (bi-encoders); ColBERT keeps token-level embeddings and a maximum-similarity function; re-rankers apply BERT cross-attention over first-stage output at high computational overhead.","lines 133-135")
E("E047","observation","Authors' summary of findings: no single approach consistently outperforms others on all datasets; in-domain performance does not correlate well with zero-shot capability; computationally expensive models (re-ranking, late interaction) perform best; efficient dense/sparse approaches can substantially underperform BM25; BM25 remains a strong baseline.","line 122; abstract line 7",notes="'we find that no single approach consistently outperforms other approaches on all datasets. Further, we notice that the in-domain performance of a model does not correlate well with its generalization capabilities: models fine-tuned with identical training data might generalize differently.'")
E("E048","dataset","The README lists 17 preprocessed benchmark datasets; four datasets (BioASQ, Signal-1M (RT), TREC-NEWS, Robust04) are marked not public and come with reproduction instructions.","README lines 82,343-349",path=R,value={"count":17},quantity="num_preprocessed_datasets_readme",notes="Different quantity from the 18 datasets of the paper (E001): preprocessed-and-downloadable versus benchmark members.")
E("E049","dataset","Licences: authors of 4 of the 19 datasets (NFCorpus, FiQA-2018, Quora, Climate-Fever) report no licence; TREC-NEWS, Robust04 and BioASQ archives are under copyright; others carry licences listed in Appendix E.","lines 885-886")

